 According to the grammar, we have:
   - A cell declaration: cell <name> <type> = <init> { <body> }
   - The body of the cell can have a tick (which is the only writer) and a [K
view (which is pure read and returns an expression).

 We are to have:
   cell ticks of type int<16> with initial value 0.
   view even that returns 1 when ticks is even.

 Since the grammar allows multiple statements in the body, we can have both[4D[K
both a tick and a view.

 However, note the rules:
   R1: single-writer: only the owning cell's tick may assign to it.
   R2: view purity: no effect assignments or propose signals inside view.

 We'll write the cell and then the view inside its body.

 The example program structure is:

 cell ticks int<16> = 0 {
   tick {
     ticks <= ticks + 1;
   }
   view parity = {
